 Thank you.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:59.980
 Thank you.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:29.980
 Thank you.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:52.180
 All right, it is 4 o'clock.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.260
 As soon as I get everybody's attention.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.040
 I should.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.400
 You guys ready?

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:06.840
 Ready as you are.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:07.780
 All right.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.160
 Do you need a baton, do you?

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.840
 I've got a next one.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.340
 Yeah, I need more than that.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:12.780
 I got one.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.200
 We're rolling.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:15.720
 All right.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.640
 It's 4 o'clock, according to this clock.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.180
 And we'll go ahead and call this work session to order and get started.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.660
 And the first thing on our agenda is a discussion.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.560
 I propose an update to the annexation policy, Area 7, and Mr. Brandon.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:42.340
 It seems like we were just here discussing this, because we were.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:53.620
 So we've had a few things develop in the sense that time.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:03:01.200
 So if you'll remember, at the end of February, or excuse me, the end of the year, 1st of December-ish,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.600
 end of November, we approved a new annexation plan.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.760
 And since that time, a few things have developed.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.320
 I'll remind you of this.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.480
 As soon as I can get my screen up, I'll show you the.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.280
 There we go.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.520
 I think it's coming.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:25.520
 All right.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.540
 So this is what we got approved.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.380
 And this was six areas.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:49.500
 Since that time, we have had, there's resources that we've become aware of, obviously.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:59.240
 There's some potential strategic resources that may be of value to the area, to the region,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:00.880
 really, to the country.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.560
 And so what this proposal is, is to add an Area 7.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.420
 That area, and I'm going to take you down here, and I'll back up here in a second,

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.140
 but just to give you a little context.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:20.040
 So on the, let me pop this up here.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:28.180
 So old map, without Area 7, proposed Area 7 right there on the right-hand side.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.440
 That's really all this is about, is adding Area 7.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.580
 That's land that's owned by the state of Utah.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.060
 There's some federal lands in there, and a handful of other landowners.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:51.700
 And so this process, we're going through a process with, in taking this back through.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.540
 There's no way to, like, amend an annexation plan.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.860
 There's not an amended process to amend your annexation plan.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.300
 So we're just taking it back through, which means we have to take it to affected entities.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.300
 We have to have two planning commission meetings, and then a council meeting.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.760
 And so we've had our affected entity meeting.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.420
 Didn't hear back from anybody yet.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.200
 We're currently in a 10-day period where we can continue to receive feedback from affected entities.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.440
 Haven't seen, haven't heard anything, but we're still in that window.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:40.220
 So, again, annexation plans are just our signal of saying, hey, this land is land that we're willing to consider annexation proposals for.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.920
 So we're not saying this, all these orange areas are necessarily coming to the city.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.300
 We're just saying, hey, we'd consider proposals.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.980
 And then you'll remember, may remember, it's not just a map.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.800
 An annexation policy plan is a decision-making guide.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.860
 Oftentimes, I guess, maybe it's just me, but I often think of the map.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:18.580
 But there's actual criteria and some considerations that are in there that help you to, that helps the council and the city make decisions about which land to bring in.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:27.080
 So, for instance, you know, a potential reason, we list potential reasons why we'd bring something in.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:34.060
 And one of those would be, well, maybe it's economically, economically, an economic value.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:37.200
 This land would be economically valuable to the city to have in.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:46.400
 Maybe it helps us make some transportation connections and add some land that has some strategic value.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.380
 We also identify in there some state code issues that we need to be aware of,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:07:00.500
 like if we're bumping up against Camp Williams or an airport, we might remind the user of the annexation plan that,

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:04.600
 hey, you need to be looking out for Camp Williams if you're going to annex land.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.580
 There's some additional considerations that need to be evaluated.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.380
 Also, you know, our city is unique.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:23.080
 And so we've identified a handful of issues that need to be considered, evaluated when we go through an annexation policy plan.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:30.420
 Remember, we have six areas and, or six current areas.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:36.720
 And then area one to the, area one and two are both to the south.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:45.500
 You can see that, I think this is good land for energy development and utility and transportation, corridor preservation.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:53.380
 And then area three is a little piece that may be valuable for open space and recreation.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:08:06.600
 Area four and five are, could be economic, land that could be very important for economic development, transportation connections.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.040
 Five, similar thing, but there may be some recreation opportunities there.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:21.280
 And six is land that may be important for recreation and open space.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:27.280
 So most of this land's already been, a lot of it's been in our annexation plan for a while.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:34.540
 Areas two and three, one, two, and three were, are new as of the end of last year.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:39.040
 And then, of course, area seven is this new area that we propose.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.560
 Like area one, it gives you access to, some more access to Utah Lake.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:53.140
 But it also, there, there's some mineral deposits on area seven that are, that are valuable.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:06.380
 And we, we have some potential property owners who are willing to, to work with us a little bit to, to, to maybe make that worth bringing into the city.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:17.280
 So, obviously, uh, when you bring something in, there's benefits, you know, maybe there's some strategic value to bringing seven in with, with minerals.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:24.020
 But then there's the cost of extending infrastructure and the environmental considerations with, with being right by Utah Lake.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:34.160
 So, um, it's not too, we're generally not hitting, hitting, you know, you can see Fairfield's annexation plan.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.180
 We're talking clear down here.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:43.860
 Um, Saratoga ends just a little pat, their, their annexation plan ends a little past Pelican Point.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:53.400
 And then you can see Cedar Fort's not really a consideration, but, or at least they, they're, they're north and we're talking quite a way south.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:58.400
 So, um, this area seven, Pelican Point's over here.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.060
 So, we're still quite a ways away from, from Saratoga's boundaries.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:19.640
 So, just, uh, uh, it's just, just wanted to give you a heads up because we've already had, we've, we've taken, we've had an affected entity meeting, public hearing, and then we're going to take this to the planning commission again for their recommendation next week.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:32.700
 And then we, we plan to bring this to you July 7th and just wanted to make sure that you were aware of it and to give you a chance to give us any feedback before, um, before we come to you, uh, in a policy session.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:48.200
 And when you went through the document, you saw that just like, uh, the areas one through six, we give a little description of, of the area and, and why it may, you know, some of the challenges and constraints that, that come with bringing area into the land.

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.800
 So, or area into the city, potentially.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:11:02.640
 So, um, you can, I've given you a document that kind of shows the, the, the, the relatively few changes to the, the existing document and just here to see if you have any feedback.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:08.640
 Or, um, anything you'd like to, um, staff to consider while we're working on this.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:12.400
 Mayor, can I just ask a question?

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:23.200
 Are there any financial obligations of when we annex these properties into Eagle Mountain that exist today that we would be taking over that we need to consider financially or from a budget perspective?

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.420
 Um, not that I'm aware of.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:38.800
 I mean, obviously, if they come in, then we, we have, uh, we start dealing with, uh, as they come in, I don't have that open right now.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:52.740
 There, obviously, there are, um, utilities and so forth that, that may come into being, but if, if, if it's land that hasn't been developed, it just stays there.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:12:01.960
 It, it, it, it starts to go through the development process before, you know, before it, uh, becomes much of a concern for us.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:09.660
 Like, what, what I'm thinking about is, is, uh, is Wasatch, Wing and Clay, is that currently in Eagle Mountain or is that just outside?

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:10.660
 It's outside.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:15.300
 So the road that leads there, would we then take over the responsibility for maintaining that road?

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.340
 That, that kind of stuff is what I'm thinking about.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:30.560
 We do have, uh, an arrangement on that road already, uh, kind of a joint arrangement, but, yeah, if, if, if, you know, land like that comes in, it, it, it does become more of our, it becomes our responsibility.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.960
 We could have our own port to Utah Lake.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:34.020
 Yeah.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:34.720
 It's like.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:44.920
 I, this may be, uh, Marcus or Evan question, but if any of this land has an RDA project on it,

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.820
 does the RDA automatically transfer to us?

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.120
 Do we have to enter into an interlocal agreement if that happens?

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:52.860
 What typically happens with that?

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:59.040
 So if there's an existing RDA with the county, then that RDA would continue to exist, but it wouldn't be with the city.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:12:59.980
 It'd be with the county.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.400
 We wouldn't necessarily have to.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:14.060
 We could, we could work with the property owner to incorporate, um, the incentives that were given to them by the county into, but as of yet, we're not a participant in that.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:17.580
 So that would have to be something that would, we'd have to affirmatively amend.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:25.420
 Okay.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:26.580
 Any other questions?

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:34.000
 Is there, I guess maybe I just wanted to state to you that this is the annexation policy plan of where we intend to go in the future,

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:44.820
 but an actual annexation itself typically would have an annexation agreement by which you would, you know, have a little bit more control and stipulate and how you apply zoning and all that type of thing.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:46.440
 All that could come at a later date.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:47.620
 This really is just the plan.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:48.060
 Right.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:49.000
 Exactly.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:51.900
 Okay.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:52.880
 Thanks, Brandon.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:56.720
 While you're up there, we can roll on to the next one of the transition lines in the ag zone.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:57.180
 Yep.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:58.460
 I'm ready to go.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:07.560
 So a few weeks ago, um, we had WSP and Rocky Mountain, uh, representatives from Rocky Mountain.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:20.760
 They were looking at the, uh, they, they continue to evaluate this transmission, uh, transmission project from Spanish fork Canyon, uh, over to the Mercer substation down by meta.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:27.400
 And, uh, and so they came in and just kind of gave us an update of where that's at.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:41.320
 Um, you can see on the map that, uh, they, they do have this blue lines, kind of their preferred route and I'll, I'll zoom in here now so you can get a little better look at that.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:59.260
 They, they have, I think there's like, what is it, uh, uh, uh, three other, uh, um, alignment, uh, other alignments that they could go with, but this is their preferred alignment.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:17.700
 And so what we noticed, uh, here, here's a little bit better, here, here's their preferred alignment right here and you can see it on a parcel map and, and kind of in comparison to, uh, to this is our ag, this is our ag zoning down in that same area.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:32.320
 So, um, so we, a lot of this land, a lot of this land where this will come through is, uh, is ag.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:41.040
 So it looks like that's probably not even in the city, but as it comes, enters into this part, into this land right here, it's, it's ag.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:52.020
 And that creates a little bit of a challenge or a lot of a challenge because, um, our current ag zone doesn't allow for, it allows for public and private utilities.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:58.440
 But if you look at the definition of utilities, it, uh, it doesn't mention anything about electrical transmission.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:09.480
 And, and so, um, we, we're here to just kind of let you know about this issue, uh, that we don't have an, a lot.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:22.380
 We don't have an ability to allow this transmission line through, uh, through our city in this area to get over to the substation because we're, transmission lines aren't allowed in the ag zone.

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:29.540
 So, um, I noticed, let me show you this.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:35.040
 We don't allow transmission zones specifically in too many areas.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:37.000
 This is, this is all I could find.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:49.780
 We allow electrical substations and power transmission lines in the LMD, the MEC, and the office, or actually, excuse me, just the LMD zone, uh, by special use.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:55.900
 So, we, we, we are, we've got data centers, they need power.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:58.680
 We just have a little bit of a hiccup in our code.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:17.840
 Um, and I know this issue's come up before and just wanted to, to bring that before the council to, to, uh, discuss this with you and see what we can do to, uh, help facilitate the completion of, uh, to, to help facilitate this transmission line, bringing power to the data centers.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:27.640
 So, what, what would you propose, are you proposing like a transit, transmission line overlay, or what, what are you proposing?

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:32.220
 Um, I am looking for, I'm open to suggestions.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.140
 I think a transmission line is something we've considered.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:47.440
 Um, we could also, rather than make a new zone, we could just add some additional standards to the, to the, we could just make it a special use.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:52.160
 In, um, let me open this up here.

00:17:54.460 --> 00:18:02.120
 We can make it a special use in the, uh, in the ag zone and then add some additional requirements.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:13.440
 This is, this is kind of our junk drawer code, our junk drawer chapter, where we've got all kinds of, uh, spare codes and all kinds of things.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:26.580
 But we could add some additional requirements to kind of beef, beef, beef up that, uh, beef up the, uh, um, requirements and, and, uh, kind of the restrictions on that use.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:32.960
 So that's, that's kind of where I'd like, I personally like the most simple solution possible.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:44.920
 Um, and overlays, we can look at an overlay, but I'd really proud, probably prefer to just add some standards to the, to 1775 to our special, special uses chapter.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:46.380
 That makes sense.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.320
 I think we're probably going to have more of this.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:55.320
 I was really excited to see this, that, uh, you know, the ability to create power, it's the substations that we're lacking.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:55.980
 Yeah.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:57.760
 The ability to distribute power.

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:01.000
 So, I think I'd support adding to the junk drawer.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:05.360
 My question stems from property tax.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:18.640
 If, is, does our property tax collection change depending upon whether or not these power lines are in ag or some other zoning?

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:23.060
 Does it, do we get less property tax if it's an ag?

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:25.240
 Is that the actual use?

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:34.880
 Is that just the base use and that's why it's ag or is there actual agricultural uses being used underneath and around these transition lines?

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:46.500
 That'd be my assumption, but I would really want to check with, with, uh, my neighbor, Diana Garcia, who runs, uh, kind of the green belt tax, uh, program for the county.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:55.420
 I, I, I'm not aware that, that, that, that messes up that, that ability to have green belt, but, um, that's something we'd have to explore a little further.

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:56.240
 Yeah.

00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:01.300
 I, I think I just want to better understand the ramifications financially for us as a city.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:10.400
 Um, if we don't have to provide any services like normal agricultural land use, then it's probably okay.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:26.680
 But if there's additional things that we have to provide as a city and eggs being used for the junk drawer principle, then I want to look at that and make sure that we're zoning it the most appropriate zone for that land use.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:33.880
 You can accomplish it with a special use.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.440
 That's the easiest way to deal with this.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:36.280
 Yeah.

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:37.480
 Okay.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:42.700
 But my question on the special use is what's our ability to deny in areas that we don't want it?

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.580
 Because I think if the special use goes in there, there's certain criteria.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.440
 If they meet the criteria, we can't deny it.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:50.540
 So it's effectively just adding it in there.

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:51.980
 Right.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:56.920
 If they meet the criteria, that, that is the downside to, to adding it as a special use.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:02.920
 The, the overlay, that's always, that's, that's kind of the thing with zones in any situation.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:12.240
 A zone gives you, if you have the ability to approve a zone, it gives you the right, uh, gives you an extra chance to look at it before it goes in.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.020
 So an overlay does give you more protection.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:17.460
 My concern is exactly what Councilman Whiting just addressed.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:20.060
 And I'm just wondering why we can't address this through a DA.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:28.820
 I mean, that gives the city council very strict parameters that they can put in place of location, time, scale, all of that.

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:30.740
 And to me, that makes the most sense.

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:32.820
 So why not put that in the special use then?

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:35.460
 But this can be granted by development agreement.

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:38.580
 Uh, I think that's the mechanism that allows us to.

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:44.140
 So if Rocky Mountain Power is, is putting up transmission lines, they're not going to agree to a development agreement.

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:45.460
 That's really just not how they work.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:49.060
 So, so how, so how do we, how do we, how do we get something like that in place?

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:51.200
 I mean, they're going to tell you, these are what we want.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:56.820
 If you want the transmission lines and there can be some room, well, room for negotiation, but that's really it.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.640
 Like if the zoning doesn't allow it, then it doesn't allow it.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.680
 That's, they're not going to like give concessions to the city.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:05.860
 That's not my experience with Rocky Mountain Power anyways.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:09.840
 Can we, can we create a specific zone that would allow just that period?

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:13.080
 I mean, that was a council member Huish's first suggestion.

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:15.000
 Well, he's talking about an overlay.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:16.500
 I'm talking about a specific zone.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:20.620
 So they've got to come and get a zoning designation change to be able to put it in.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:22.240
 Well, typically they're going to get an easement, right?

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.440
 They're not going to buy the property in a few simple.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:28.660
 That's right.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:33.480
 I think the overlay may be cleaner because you could just put the overlay exactly where you want it.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:36.440
 They're still going to have an easement.

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:38.460
 That's why I said the special use makes the most sense.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:40.780
 You can still deal with this in a contract, Marcus.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.040
 Let's have to set the parameters of what that is.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:46.660
 And maybe they're going to say this, this, this, and this have to be in there.

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:47.860
 And maybe that's the way that is.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:55.940
 But you can also be restrictive in saying we're not going to allow you to do, you know, the 100-foot transmission lines right down the middle of this corner.

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:58.460
 Yeah, well, that's what we would put in the special use standards, right?

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:07.820
 So you'd say, you know, it's proximity to residential zoning, you know, height of the lines, things like that could be put into the special use standards.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.300
 So you don't have to do it ad hoc every time.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:10.660
 Yeah.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.640
 I would prefer just bracket it.

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:15.460
 I think we can do that or we don't want it there at all.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:17.440
 I mean, that's the decision, right?

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:19.300
 Put the brackets in place or say we don't have access.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:23.160
 Yeah, I mean, I think that was the suggestion was to put standards in place that we can live with.

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:31.540
 So that way, if people are, if they're putting it in, you know, so there's certain areas of town that's usually going to be related to proximity to existing residential.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:38.160
 I mean, maybe there are other reasons, proximity to other types of uses like schools or something that might be an issue.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:42.300
 But I think that's, you know, those kinds of standards can be put in a special use code.

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:51.100
 Do you know what the standard, I mean, I think you want to have at least some distance, especially if you're dealing with the large overhead lines from houses.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:54.440
 And I know that there's some cities that let them literally go right over the top of the houses.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:58.220
 My brother lives next to some in Bluffdale, which is crazy to me.

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:02.160
 But I think that's probably the main concern is to just.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:12.900
 I mean, I will tell you that there were existing transmission lines running through what is now the street that my house is on and run through the park across the street from my house.

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:16.080
 It's, I mean, nobody seems to think it's a big deal.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:20.840
 So, I mean, as far as a safety concern, you know, aesthetically sure.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:26.920
 Yeah, I mean, they run through Alpine homes in Lehigh.

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:32.120
 I mean, every set of those I've dealt with, people trying to buy the houses or sell the houses with that.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:33.780
 So, they do exist.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.580
 The question is, what do we care about?

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:38.340
 What is it that we're trying to prevent?

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:40.020
 What is it that we're trying to bracket?

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:46.440
 Maybe that's, maybe get some feedback from people who feel strongly about it not being this or not being that.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:48.960
 We put that into special use, right?

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:55.200
 Because even if you do an overlay, it's, you're going to have to apply it somewhere.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.360
 And they're going to come in and say, we want to put it right here.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:24:59.740
 And you're going to be like, I don't want it there.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.500
 And they'll ask for the overlay zone.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:05.100
 It's better just to bracket it and say where you want it.

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:10.720
 So, well, wouldn't the overlay do that, though?

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:12.680
 That's an after-the-fact thing, though.

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:17.380
 The overlay, unless you're applying it and saying this is the place where transmission lines can go,

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:21.540
 they're going to be coming in and asking for the overlay zone of where they want to put it.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.440
 So, either you're going to have to make the decision beforehand,

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.800
 or you might as well just zone it at that point and say,

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:30.300
 transmission lines can go through this corridor that we've created through the city,

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:32.960
 but we're not going to allow them to go anywhere else.

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:37.780
 I think part of the problem with that is I don't have a crystal ball to understand

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:40.440
 where transmission lines might need to go throughout the city, right?

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:41.240
 Long term.

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:44.500
 So, that, for me, is why the overlay makes a lot more sense

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:47.700
 because it's going to require a council decision to say,

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:49.700
 yes, we're going to apply the overlay zone here.

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:54.700
 I think those special standards should apply and should be part of that overlay zone.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:26:00.620
 And I guess the concern that I have is maybe there needs to be a tiered approach

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:04.060
 because we might want some big transmission lines on the south end of the city

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:05.240
 to support the data center.

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:10.980
 We certainly don't want them on some of the foothills or some of those areas that we're trying to preserve

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:18.480
 just for some unique, perhaps, residential or even commercial uses.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:21.280
 So, I think that's where we need to be contemplative about this.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:26.080
 Do you have other cities' codes related to this that you've looked at?

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:27.020
 Not yet.

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:27.340
 No.

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.180
 I think that might be a good starting point.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:30.460
 Let's see what other people are doing.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:32.100
 I know Lehigh has them.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:33.160
 Bluffdale has them.

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:35.040
 West Valley has them.

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:37.040
 Basically, West Jordan.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:39.800
 I think Harriman.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:41.800
 So, you could pretty much follow that corridor.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:46.100
 Somebody's going to have something where they're addressing, even just in a standard.

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:47.780
 Maybe there's not a zone in there.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.180
 Maybe that's a starting point for us to take a look at.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:51.020
 Yeah.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:52.560
 We'll definitely research all that.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:53.700
 But I think I would say this, Brandon.

00:26:53.700 --> 00:27:00.620
 And I appreciate that you brought this to our attention because I, for one, at least, am willing and open to helping find a solution

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.960
 so that we can get that transmission line to that substation.

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:05.080
 I want that to be clear.

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:06.480
 I will.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:07.140
 Sorry.

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:14.620
 I really like the idea of the overlay, specifically because of the public noticing requirements

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:18.800
 and the public hearing that would have to take place when they come to request that.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:25.800
 And I think, to everyone's point, letting the public weigh in on that, I think, is important.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:28.000
 It gives us an awareness of how it's going to impact them.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:36.500
 And my other question was, we have those transmission lines that go through the ranches at Liberty Farms,

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.920
 and then it comes over Lake Mountain, and then we have the ones that are at Woodhaven.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:45.300
 Would the ones that we're considering for the Mercer substation be larger or smaller than those?

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:51.700
 On their website, they gave a range for how large those would be,

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:58.200
 and it was somewhere in the neighborhood of, yeah, they gave a range.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.600
 I think it was like 90 to 120.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:02.080
 I'm trying to remember.

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:07.920
 Does anyone know off the top of their head how tall some of those other ones are that we have going through?

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:10.340
 This is a major transmission line.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:14.100
 It's the exact same thing as the other lines that go through the high voltage.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:19.120
 So, like, the Woodhaven one and, like, maybe not Woodhaven, but for sure the Liberty Farms ones.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:20.400
 The ones that go down Lake Mountain.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:21.140
 Lake Mountain, yeah.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:28.380
 Like, and the thing is, is I don't know that we had any say about where those were going.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:33.900
 We just developed around them after the fact, and we're mindful of that.

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:39.320
 And our code specifically says that you can't have structures under power corridors.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:43.180
 So, like, I think in a lot of ways that is a protection for us.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:50.480
 But I still think the best way to handle it is the overlay, because it does have that public hearing process.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:29:02.420
 So, my last question is, I know that along Lake Mountain Road, on the west side of it, we already have power lines going.

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:06.420
 So, we can't run these new power lines close to those?

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:22.040
 It's possible we could look at that, but they, I think they're looking for the most expeditious route, and they're only entering in.

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:27.640
 They're entering basically right through there.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:44.360
 Is there any other questions?

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:44.780
 Go ahead.

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:48.620
 So, what you're saying is they're just going to come in two of those blocks.

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:50.260
 Are those 160-acre blocks?

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.660
 Those look like sections to me.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:53.000
 Yeah.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.560
 So, um, 160 acres.

00:29:56.700 --> 00:29:57.540
 Right, exactly.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:08.180
 But I'll take that information.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:11.080
 I'll research what other cities are doing.

00:30:13.220 --> 00:30:17.860
 Generally, is there, I'm getting the sense that the council would prefer an overlay.

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:19.680
 Is that what I'm hearing?

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:23.860
 I'm seeing foreheads nodding.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:25.720
 Yes.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:34.860
 If that's the case, then I can pursue that and look at what other cities are doing.

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:39.640
 I'll bring some options, but I'll be leaning towards an overlay, it looks like.

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:42.920
 So, try, we like simple solutions.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:47.780
 I'll try to make it fairly simple while respecting the idea that it's an overlay.

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:52.320
 One last really quick question, because I have a feeling residents are going to want to know this.

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.160
 Sometimes people think that our rates are going to want to know this.

00:30:55.160 --> 00:31:02.440
 Sometimes people think that our rates are going up because these transmission lines are going in to take care of specific large-use customers.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:17.520
 If there's any information that we as a city can obtain and share with our residents down the road about whether or not that's a reality or if that's just the rumor that's going around, I think that would be useful information for us to share with our residents.

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:25.320
 I'll, yes, and we can, what we can do is I can work with our communication team and put this website out.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:35.500
 The other thing I'll let you know is we do have some representatives from WSP and Rocky Mountain that are likely watching us right now.

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:40.100
 And I can, I know that I told them I was going to come and talk to you.

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:48.080
 So, they'll be able to provide me some additional information as well, but we'll help to get the word out.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:50.060
 All right.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:50.860
 Thank you.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:55.940
 The next discussion, 1C, is the Sage Valley Community Entrance Sign.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:00.200
 Mr. Stephen Lemons has some information.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:27.220
 All right.

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:42.180
 So, we received an application at the beginning of the year from the Sage Valley community wanting to put in a community entrance sign similar to what you see in, I'd say, the majority of neighborhoods here in the city, especially in the ranches.

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:54.080
 It says it's a fairly uniform sign there, the proposals to put it between what used to be airport or airport road there and the current or the new alignment.

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:57.300
 So, it says going to be right here with this orange rectangle.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:00.660
 So, the intersection of airport road and Whelan drive.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:14.640
 This is what was provided by the applicant, so put it right there outside of would miss the storm light or the storm drain here outside of the site triangle.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:23.460
 This is all we've gotten so far from the applicant is these mock-up drawings of what it would look like, the sizes.

00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:27.300
 The code doesn't really speak to what the size has to be.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:31.500
 All it says is that there has to be an agreement with the city to put it on city property.

00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:33.720
 It has to be leased.

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:37.020
 But outside of that, there's no specific thing.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:42.760
 So, really, the recommendations from staff is we just want to get feedback from the council.

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:46.320
 Is that something the council is willing to pursue, what they want to see in an agreement.

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:50.640
 So, we take it back to the applicant and go forward with that.

00:33:52.620 --> 00:34:01.080
 Just to add some color, I mentioned that this was going to be on the agenda, and I'll have to recuse myself from any vote here because this is my neighborhood.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:03.020
 I think I have to recuse myself.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:03.980
 Maybe I don't.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:06.180
 I don't benefit.

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:07.220
 It's just for the neighborhood.

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:08.420
 It's a discussion, anyway.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:09.000
 It's a discussion.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:09.740
 We're not voting on anything.

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:21.800
 So, back before I moved into this neighborhood, Patterson, part of their punch list from the city was to create a monument sign.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:24.260
 Again, there was one that was built at one point.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:25.660
 That was before I moved in.

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:35.500
 And my understanding is when the utilities came in for another part of the neighborhood, that monument sign was removed.

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:42.260
 So, I don't know when monument signs are created, if they're given to the city to kind of maintain or manage.

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:46.220
 But that monument sign was not ever replaced.

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:50.280
 So, I just wanted to add that for some context for the council.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:53.940
 And I have a neighbor that has the original MDA, the punch list.

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:57.900
 He's got all the documentation, which I don't know if you've seen any of that.

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.200
 But there was once upon a time a sign.

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:01.060
 It was removed.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:02.240
 It hasn't been added back.

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:14.060
 So, I think the question is, my question is, is there a responsibility on the city's part, if that was a city sign, to replace it?

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:17.560
 And if so, is there a budget for that?

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:19.660
 The neighborhood's willing to build the sign.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:22.740
 They have the technical abilities to do that.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:25.920
 The cost to build it is about $3,500 in materials.

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:33.080
 In general, to answer that question, the city's been taking those out because they've been in the right-of-ways.

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.640
 Like, for instance, in Cedar Pass Ranch, they're right in the middle of the road.

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:39.160
 And as we're widening the roads, we're taking them out and not replacing them.

00:35:40.120 --> 00:35:41.740
 Throughout the city, that's been the case.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.620
 When we were a lot smaller, maybe they worked.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:50.900
 But we've taken out multiple monument signs, mostly throughout the ranches.

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:52.180
 That's where they were prominent.

00:35:52.820 --> 00:35:54.660
 And they're not being replaced.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:36:02.740
 I would say if they want them, you're an HOA, right?

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:03.660
 We're not.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:07.480
 Well, maybe somebody's personal property can be put on that.

00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:10.900
 If you put it in the right-of-way, there's a good chance it'll be taken down again, it sounds like.

00:36:13.580 --> 00:36:14.600
 The location.

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:16.900
 Oh.

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:20.540
 Can you pull the map back up what's being considered?

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:28.620
 So that's the sidewalk that goes up to the middle school.

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.980
 And I don't know that anything would be developed there.

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:36.160
 There's just a storm drain in that area right now.

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:46.700
 Zach, one of the things that I, when we were reviewing this as a staff, you'll see the little sidewalk right here.

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:48.040
 I'm pointing on the other side.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:52.640
 And then there's a little yellow line right there.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:58.640
 So this triangle is city property as well, but less likely to have right-of-way.

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:03.940
 This portion, that right-of-way, could go all the way to that yellow line.

00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:16.000
 So if the council was interested, I would at least propose, if you don't want it taken out, you know, a few feet further north is less likely to be impacted.

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:23.740
 But right where that sidewalk is and that radius is part of what could be future road design or maybe not.

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:25.540
 You're saying, oh, to move it north?

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:26.140
 Yeah.

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:28.220
 I mean, on this side, you can see a little bit better.

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:30.440
 Just a few more feet from here is there.

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:33.900
 This is less likely to be impacted than here.

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:43.320
 If the council chooses to do something like this, and a lease would still be part of that, however this proceeds.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:49.780
 But in general, a lot of the monument signs have been removed and not replaced.

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:52.960
 I'm supportive of the monument signs.

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:58.640
 It creates a sense of place, a sense of arrival, a sense of home, a sense of ownership.

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:00.760
 I think we shouldn't be removing them.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.780
 Obviously, if we need to move them, that's one thing.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:05.600
 But removing them, I don't think we should do.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:07.140
 I think we should have them.

00:38:07.180 --> 00:38:09.160
 And I'm in favor of Sage Valley doing this.

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:12.460
 And I'll speak in here.

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:18.700
 I mean, it sounds like the residents would be willing to take the financial burden on themselves.

00:38:18.820 --> 00:38:20.040
 That's what I'm hearing you say, Zach.

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:20.400
 Yes.

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:20.720
 Is that right?

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:21.180
 Yep.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:25.680
 So really, they're just asking the city for permission to be able to locate it on city property.

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:26.260
 Right.

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:28.120
 And that would require some sort of an agreement.

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:31.820
 I think that's what staff is saying their recommendation is that we consider that.

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:34.740
 And I'm certainly open to that.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:42.980
 I mean, we have to talk about if and when it needs to be removed, and there's a likelihood that that could happen at some point in time.

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:51.300
 I think we need to be realistic enough to know that that road's going to expand, it's going to get bigger, and that right-of-way is likely to change.

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:54.200
 Maybe not in my lifetime, but probably in yours, Zach.

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:57.640
 So assuming we're both still around for a while.

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:04.120
 But I'm open to that, especially if the residents are willing to take this project on themselves.

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:07.840
 I think if it put a burden on the city's finances, I might feel differently.

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:13.020
 But where the residents are saying, look, we're willing to come and put some sweat equity in this, it's important to us.

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:14.800
 I agree with what Councilmember Whiting said.

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:17.000
 It does create a sense of place.

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:19.800
 And I'm supportive also of what the mayor said.

00:39:19.860 --> 00:39:23.720
 I understand why we're taking them out of the middle of those road networks.

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:30.660
 And it's very difficult to get any type of vehicle, a truck and a trailer, and anything bigger than that becomes very, very difficult.

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:32.980
 And so would I like to see those relocated?

00:39:32.980 --> 00:39:36.740
 The answer is yes, but it's not been practical in those areas where they moved.

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:48.860
 What would the maintenance entail, and would that be something that the neighborhood takes on by agreement or the city would have some responsibility for?

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:50.360
 Do you know about that?

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:57.400
 I think the neighborhood would, I can't speak for future neighbors, but those that are there today would maintain it.

00:39:58.560 --> 00:40:04.100
 It would be made out of steel and rock, cement, so I don't think there would be much maintenance.

00:40:04.820 --> 00:40:08.560
 There is a post hole in the back of the sign for a flagpole.

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:10.860
 So it would be maintained by the neighborhood.

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:24.380
 I think my point of reference when I ask about maintenance is in the ranches, almost all of those neighborhoods got some sort of monument sign that was steel, rock, cement.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:32.980
 And there's probably not a lot of maintenance, but after a while the letters on the monument start to look shabby.

00:40:32.980 --> 00:40:39.660
 And I think because the ranch's HOA was dissolved, the cities had to take on that maintenance.

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:48.700
 And I think if the neighbors in those neighborhoods knew what they could do to maintain it, they probably would step up and do it.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:55.940
 But it's just clear expectations of who's supposed to do what with it, I think is important.

00:40:55.940 --> 00:41:11.660
 And I think if we agree to do this as a city, I think we'll see more neighborhoods wanting to do something like that because, to Council Member Whiting's point, it does create a sense of place.

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:15.340
 It does create that kind of, like, ownership of something.

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:17.700
 So I'm in favor of the idea.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:21.220
 I just want to make sure all of our I's are dotted and our T's are crossed.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:24.680
 So you raise a couple of really good points, Council Member Clark.

00:41:24.820 --> 00:41:28.640
 And so one of the things I'm sitting here thinking about is the continuity of design.

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:32.140
 Are some going to have flagpoles and some aren't?

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:36.500
 Are they going to be out of steel letters, stainless steel letters?

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:38.320
 Are they going to be painted fluorescent pink?

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:39.440
 What are they going to look like?

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:41.180
 Depends on the neighborhood.

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:52.220
 And so I think that if we're thinking about something like this, I think we should write and create some standards so that there's some continuity in the design and in the application of that citywide.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:58.320
 We have standards for monument signs already, so I think it would probably just have to follow that.

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:02.500
 And maybe we look at that and tighten it up and make sure that applies.

00:42:02.700 --> 00:42:06.720
 And the next thing that went through my head as you were speaking is what happens when a car hits this?

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:09.640
 Who's going to take care of that?

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:11.440
 Who's going to bear the liability of?

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:22.340
 Existing city code would require that the applicant take on that burden of maintenance, of basement, liability, indemnification.

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:28.660
 The problem really becomes, and it would be a lease of the city's property.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:30.400
 So who's going to lease it?

00:42:30.400 --> 00:42:31.620
 One individual homeowner?

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:34.180
 Or is it going to be, you don't have a homeowner's association.

00:42:34.660 --> 00:42:35.380
 So, you know.

00:42:35.380 --> 00:42:37.860
 It would be the applicant.

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:38.840
 Okay.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:41.060
 So that's a pretty heavy lift.

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:43.780
 You know, I don't know that I would sign that.

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:47.780
 And so the problem becomes, of course, what happens when somebody moves.

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:52.080
 I mean, we can say, look, you got to either sign the lease or we're taking it out.

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:58.820
 I don't know how realistic that is, to be honest, from the city's perspective.

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:05.080
 So in neighborhoods today, I mean, there's a lot of these monument signs throughout the ranches.

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:13.060
 Each one of those monument signs, there is somebody in that neighborhood that currently has a legal financial responsibility?

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:18.760
 My suspicion is that that was part of a common area and the HOA at the time.

00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:21.940
 And now there's no HOA, so the city owns the signs.

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:25.840
 So, like, a rival, I think they have an entrance sign?

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:30.000
 Yeah, they may have it on their property, not on city property.

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:33.360
 I don't know that for sure.

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:45.020
 So, for instance, what you wouldn't need is if you decided to put it, you know, like right here on private property, right here on private property, you know, outside of the city property.

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:47.120
 That would, those are all on private property.

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:49.260
 At least that's what he's, yeah.

00:43:52.360 --> 00:44:00.040
 Honestly, that's a safer bet, Council Member Huish, from my perspective, is to talk to those two neighbors and see if they're willing to let you construct a sign on either of those spots.

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:05.700
 That removes a bunch of concerns that are going to be raised.

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:13.220
 I mean, I see you looking at that, and I think that part of the dedication from the developer was that they put the signs in.

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:20.840
 They were responsible to build them, to install them, to dedicate them to either the HOA or the city, whichever the course was.

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:29.100
 I mean, I'm trying to think of a monument sign that's come to us in my term, and off the top of my head, I'm not thinking of one.

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:31.880
 I wasn't here.

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:33.740
 I was on planning commission.

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:35.900
 I'm not taking the heat for the Overland sign.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:52.140
 So I think, and I don't want to speak for the applicant specifically, but I think the idea is that the neighborhood would build a monument sign that would add to the culture of the city, of the neighborhood.

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:59.080
 When you enter Airport Road, there would be a nice monument sign that adds value to the city, it adds appeal.

00:44:59.780 --> 00:45:04.900
 So as a council, maybe, is that something that we want as a city?

00:45:05.200 --> 00:45:08.220
 Do we want to have that exist?

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:09.800
 Oh, sorry.

00:45:09.940 --> 00:45:12.980
 I don't think I would put that on one person to be financially responsible.

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:19.480
 I think it would be something that the neighborhood could build to code, engineered, all of that.

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:24.980
 The financial cost would be on the neighborhood to build it and put it up.

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:32.320
 Something that is high quality, but ultimately the city would own it, yeah, maybe maintain it.

00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:35.420
 I think we should, well, this is my opinion.

00:45:36.500 --> 00:45:56.880
 I think we should work to find the parameters at which we're comfortable to get this to a yes, because we're looking at right now behind people's homes, land that's the cities, and getting that sold to some of the adjoining property owners, or updating that land and putting in a different trail.

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:07.860
 We're very much in the process of beautifying our city and setting ourselves apart from other areas, and I think that this is a good step in that direction.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:17.480
 I don't know how all of it works out, and I'm sure there's going to be some legal considerations, and who's responsible for what, and all sorts of things.

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:23.180
 But I think it's worth the wrestle to try to figure it out together.

00:46:23.620 --> 00:46:30.360
 And if we go through the process and the exercise of looking at it, and it just doesn't work out, fine.

00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:33.060
 But I think it's worth trying to get it to a yes.

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:38.440
 Is there any reason we can't put it in the easement on the east side of the road?

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:40.880
 Where's my pointer at?

00:46:44.340 --> 00:46:45.820
 So in this area over here.

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:50.140
 That's where most of them are.

00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:52.180
 That's not right away.

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:53.340
 That's not in an easement.

00:46:53.620 --> 00:46:56.200
 This is considered the right-of-way right here?

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:58.860
 That's your property.

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:04.300
 Yeah, so I'm not sure if those lines are precisely accurate.

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:14.820
 But, yeah, anything to the outside of the homeowner's property area is not going to be an easement.

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:16.280
 It would be part of the right-of-way.

00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:18.880
 That's not a utility easement is what you're saying?

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.740
 I don't think so.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:24.140
 I mean, I haven't looked at this specifically.

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:31.100
 So, I mean, you would normally click on it to determine, you know, and the county records are going to tell you who owns that property.

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:33.100
 If it says Eagle Mountain City, then it's in the right-of-way.

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:37.680
 If it says something else, then it's, then obviously that's not right-of-way.

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:41.280
 It says it belongs to Eagle Mountain City.

00:47:41.520 --> 00:47:45.040
 Yeah, it's going to be part of the public right-of-way then, which is what we're talking about.

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:45.880
 It's what they're requesting.

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:54.220
 To me, it makes more sense to do it on the east side because there's less likely that they're going to come, start taking the front part of the lawn out.

00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:57.420
 I think on the opposite side of the road, it's a likely scenario.

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:03.640
 What are you talking about?

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:06.000
 It's a loop.

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:09.140
 That's the entrance off of the airport road into their neighborhood.

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:11.840
 Okay.

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:13.140
 Fair enough.

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:17.640
 But anybody that lives anywhere besides those three houses comes in and goes this way.

00:48:18.320 --> 00:48:19.340
 I've driven it.

00:48:19.960 --> 00:48:23.080
 So, it goes to these five houses, maybe.

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:24.740
 I'm saying it's not a busy road.

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:26.300
 I don't disagree.

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:31.160
 Is that going to stay that way when they do the expansion, though, of SR73?

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:35.540
 Do you know why they chose that location over the other one?

00:48:36.300 --> 00:48:38.040
 Over the house?

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:40.800
 Oh, you mean going further north?

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:43.920
 Up here.

00:48:45.920 --> 00:48:46.540
 Oh, yeah.

00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:48.560
 Yeah, nobody drives that way.

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:51.880
 So, everybody goes, they go straight through.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:55.320
 And then hook up and then connect that way.

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:56.260
 Yeah.

00:48:56.320 --> 00:49:02.220
 So, since the road pattern changed, that gets less use further north.

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:03.620
 Point.

00:49:04.260 --> 00:49:04.420
 Here.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:05.580
 Use that for point.

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:06.820
 Where does everybody go?

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:08.620
 Right through here.

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:09.100
 Okay.

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:11.440
 That's what I assumed.

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:16.160
 I knew they used to use the other entrance, but since airport road's done, now they're using the south entrance.

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:35.900
 So, to sum things up, our concerns are placement, maintenance, who owns the land that it's on, whether or not there would be a lease, whether or not the applicant is in charge of maintenance, or whether or not this was received as a gift to the city and the city takes it on maintenance-wise.

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:39.040
 I don't think we can answer most of those questions.

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:42.200
 I mean, is there anybody who's against putting it on the city property?

00:49:42.660 --> 00:49:42.860
 No.

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:46.240
 That's the first question.

00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:48.400
 I don't have a problem with that.

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:49.900
 Sounds like Zach doesn't.

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:51.200
 Sounds like Craig doesn't.

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:52.240
 Brett, where are you at?

00:49:53.420 --> 00:49:57.280
 I'm just concerned about any liability that that might bring to the city, that's all.

00:49:57.340 --> 00:49:58.180
 Oh, we already have them.

00:49:58.380 --> 00:49:59.880
 The reality is they already exist.

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:02.540
 We have monument signs all throughout the ranches.

00:50:03.740 --> 00:50:04.180
 Yes.

00:50:04.820 --> 00:50:06.400
 Should we be creating one in this area?

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:07.320
 That's really the question.

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:09.700
 Do we want to create an additional liability?

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:11.660
 That's the question I'm asking myself.

00:50:13.940 --> 00:50:15.220
 I'm not worried about that.

00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:16.840
 I mean, we already have the liability.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:18.880
 They're already there in droves.

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:22.380
 So, I'm more concerned about identification of areas.

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:26.520
 I do think that that's a benefit to be able to identify the area that you're coming to,

00:50:26.620 --> 00:50:30.080
 especially people who are trying to bring packages and things of that nature.

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:32.320
 All of those things are helpful for directions.

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:37.700
 I think less impactful on the east side of the road is my opinion.

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:42.720
 I'd say maybe they'd be smarter to work between those two houses

00:50:42.720 --> 00:50:47.700
 and see if they can get free council members to agree to put them where those white rocks are,

00:50:48.460 --> 00:50:49.680
 somewhere in that area.

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:52.140
 That makes more sense to me than putting it across the street.

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.280
 Where that's not the entrance to the neighborhood.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:51:02.820
 It sounds like there used to be a sign there that the city would have had liability for.

00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:05.260
 It got a monument there and it got removed.

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:09.980
 So, we're just adding back something the city previously already agreed to take liability for.

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:12.640
 So, I'm fine with another monument going back up there.

00:51:12.880 --> 00:51:14.180
 There was one there to begin with.

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:19.000
 I'm flexible as to the location, whether it's east or west, frankly.

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:23.100
 And I would also add, if our monument standards don't allow flags,

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:26.380
 I am supportive that a flag could be added to the monument.

00:51:27.080 --> 00:51:28.760
 So, just a couple things to point out.

00:51:28.760 --> 00:51:30.840
 Not to your opinion.

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:32.460
 I don't care about your opinion.

00:51:32.760 --> 00:51:39.340
 But it wasn't the city's.

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:43.660
 It was most likely on an HOA or private property or whatever when that got expanded.

00:51:43.760 --> 00:51:44.500
 So, not necessarily.

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:49.100
 Also, if it's on city land and it is an American flag, is it lit?

00:51:49.520 --> 00:51:51.960
 And if it's lit, who's carrying the burden for that electricity?

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:52.500
 Is that city?

00:51:53.060 --> 00:51:54.720
 And who's going to replace the flag?

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:55.900
 So, all of those questions, right?

00:51:55.960 --> 00:51:57.000
 I think you could do solar.

00:51:57.880 --> 00:51:58.980
 I don't think it would cost anything.

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:02.040
 But an American flag, I should have clarified.

00:52:02.240 --> 00:52:03.920
 Yes, an American flag only.

00:52:04.060 --> 00:52:04.180
 Yeah.

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:09.680
 And most of the stand-up flags that you put in a post, they come with a solar top.

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:11.760
 So, they project down on.

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:14.180
 There's quite a few vets in our neighborhood.

00:52:14.320 --> 00:52:15.760
 We'd make sure that would be lit up.

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:19.880
 Okay.

00:52:20.320 --> 00:52:20.860
 Go ahead.

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:23.480
 Do you feel like you've got the direction?

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:26.280
 Obviously, there's some questions that need to be asked to the applicant.

00:52:27.040 --> 00:52:29.980
 Do you feel like you have at least some beneficial questions to ask?

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:32.600
 I think I have enough direction.

00:52:32.660 --> 00:52:33.900
 I'm going to ask Marcus because you're going to be the one right.

00:52:33.900 --> 00:52:38.800
 My concern is really, like, is the city wanting to entertain a lease agreement?

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:42.800
 Or is the city wanting to own it?

00:52:44.300 --> 00:52:45.020
 I would think so.

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:49.160
 Because if they build it at their expense and install it and we want to own it just to,

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:53.100
 not put that burden on one individual, then we should say that now.

00:52:53.700 --> 00:52:58.060
 Otherwise, the code says that their code allows for somebody to lease property from the city

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.660
 and that would be an ongoing financial burden for them.

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:03.900
 If we're going to do it, then the city owns it.

00:53:04.760 --> 00:53:06.820
 Like, if they're going to pay for it and dedicate it to the city,

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:09.240
 my preference would be that the city owns it.

00:53:11.020 --> 00:53:15.480
 Yeah, I'm kind of looking at it as a gift to the city,

00:53:15.700 --> 00:53:18.920
 much like other things have been gifted to the city,

00:53:19.660 --> 00:53:22.220
 statues and different types of monuments and whatnot.

00:53:22.480 --> 00:53:27.580
 So I think once it's on our property, we take ownership of it and maintain it.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:30.000
 Agreed.

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:35.220
 I think if we're going to do it, that that's the only way to really do it for it.

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:37.860
 Because then the city controls what happens to it.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:40.780
 If it needs to be moved, it needs to be removed.

00:53:41.060 --> 00:53:42.860
 It needs to be maintained, all of the above.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:45.520
 So if that's where the council is going to land,

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:48.580
 I think that's probably a better option than trying to put that burden on one individual.

00:53:48.580 --> 00:53:51.540
 I just think that if that's the decision,

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:54.120
 the council needs to be very clear with the applicant

00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:56.940
 that there's no guarantee that sign stays there.

00:53:57.260 --> 00:53:57.340
 Right.

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:01.360
 Okay.

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:03.560
 Last comment so we can roll on.

00:54:03.680 --> 00:54:07.680
 Does the council have any recommendation on the current location,

00:54:08.060 --> 00:54:09.820
 move it further, a little bit further north?

00:54:11.780 --> 00:54:15.980
 I think you have a better chance of not having it be taken down.

00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:16.340
 Get moved again?

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:16.760
 Yeah.

00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:20.320
 Do you feel okay with that, Stephen, talking to the applicant about location?

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:21.340
 Yeah.

00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:21.720
 Okay.

00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:27.960
 And then I would just say, let's take a look at what the code is with entry monuments

00:54:27.960 --> 00:54:33.060
 and just make sure, one, that the proposed one is congruent with that,

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:37.820
 but then, two, is there anything in our code that needs to be tightened up?

00:54:37.880 --> 00:54:41.800
 Because I do think that there will be other neighborhoods that want to do this

00:54:41.800 --> 00:54:45.020
 as a gift to the city, and we should figure it out now,

00:54:45.080 --> 00:54:46.240
 and you guys can be the guinea pig.

00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:49.300
 All right.

00:54:49.300 --> 00:54:49.740
 Thanks, Stephen.

00:54:49.800 --> 00:54:51.140
 I think we better roll on.

00:54:51.280 --> 00:54:52.180
 Does that cover that?

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:53.180
 Okay.

00:54:53.400 --> 00:54:54.760
 And I know you had a comment a minute ago.

00:54:54.780 --> 00:54:55.620
 I don't know if I got to you.

00:54:55.660 --> 00:54:56.580
 You changed your mind?

00:54:57.340 --> 00:54:57.940
 I was covered.

00:54:58.180 --> 00:54:58.500
 Okay.

00:54:58.900 --> 00:54:59.220
 Perfect.

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:02.420
 Then I'll just turn it to you on the next item anyway and say,

00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:03.360
 is there any items?

00:55:04.420 --> 00:55:06.140
 I don't think I have anything.

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:10.420
 I sent a kind of a detailed email out to the council as I was flying out of town.

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:14.320
 I just appreciate the council and the staff for filling in for me the last couple days,

00:55:14.320 --> 00:55:18.020
 and I think that's all I had unless the council has questions.

00:55:18.880 --> 00:55:24.560
 Mayor, can we ask that the annexation plan presentation be added either to our packet

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:27.100
 or the minute so the images are available with the record?

00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:29.540
 Sure.

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:31.280
 Is that okay?

00:55:31.400 --> 00:55:31.580
 Okay.

00:55:31.760 --> 00:55:32.200
 Thank you.

00:55:33.320 --> 00:55:35.000
 Can you take care of that, Brandon?

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:36.880
 Yeah, absolutely.

00:55:39.020 --> 00:55:39.880
 Work with Lacey.

00:55:40.060 --> 00:55:40.400
 All right.

00:55:40.760 --> 00:55:43.860
