 All right.

01:30:05.380 --> 01:30:12.220
 The 13C is a resolution approving service agreement with West Coast Code Consultants,

01:30:12.220 --> 01:30:14.000
 the third-party billing permits.

01:30:15.200 --> 01:30:15.980
 Any questions?

01:30:16.400 --> 01:30:16.560
 No?

01:30:17.440 --> 01:30:19.000
 No, I do have a question on this.

01:30:19.100 --> 01:30:20.300
 I have a couple of questions on this.

01:30:21.320 --> 01:30:22.300
 Five-year agreement.

01:30:22.620 --> 01:30:28.180
 It seems like a really long time to contract with somebody.

01:30:28.180 --> 01:30:29.840
 I'm just curious why it's five years.

01:30:30.360 --> 01:30:33.340
 And if they want five years, I want to have them give us a discount.

01:30:33.940 --> 01:30:36.840
 If it's over like a one-year or a two-year contract.

01:30:37.340 --> 01:30:42.540
 Craig, I mean, you realize that these fees, whatever they're charging, are being paid by the developers.

01:30:43.500 --> 01:30:44.780
 I mean, this is just a plan review.

01:30:44.780 --> 01:30:45.780
 This is an alternate.

01:30:45.780 --> 01:30:51.940
 This is an additional, as I understand it, building inspection team that's going to review plans.

01:30:52.060 --> 01:30:56.520
 And so whatever those costs are, they just go right through, I think.

01:30:56.940 --> 01:31:00.820
 And then I just want to identify, too, this is not an exclusive contract.

01:31:00.820 --> 01:31:08.240
 So this is just that they're going to be an approved vendor for us, that we can utilize their services for five years.

01:31:08.360 --> 01:31:10.140
 This is what their fee schedule kind of looks like.

01:31:10.220 --> 01:31:13.840
 But we have an additional contract with another company that we do.

01:31:14.000 --> 01:31:15.160
 It is not exclusive.

01:31:15.360 --> 01:31:16.780
 It doesn't mean we couldn't use somebody else.

01:31:16.780 --> 01:31:21.860
 The attempt here is basically to make sure that somebody else doesn't get to choose who they're using.

01:31:22.180 --> 01:31:22.340
 Right.

01:31:22.340 --> 01:31:23.940
 Because that is an option with the state code.

01:31:24.140 --> 01:31:24.400
 Right.

01:31:24.600 --> 01:31:25.300
 We have multiple.

01:31:25.640 --> 01:31:27.280
 And then just the volume that we have.

01:31:27.620 --> 01:31:35.160
 So mostly for us, it's because of the volume that we have, we need more than one vendor processing these for us.

01:31:35.360 --> 01:31:36.160
 Exactly.

01:31:36.500 --> 01:31:42.560
 That's what I've seen in DRC meetings is that WC3 is overwhelmed with the volume of work they're sending to them.

01:31:42.560 --> 01:31:45.980
 And what it's doing is retarding some of those applications from getting through in a timely manner.

01:31:46.660 --> 01:31:54.440
 And so this is an assistance to really to create another firm that has that expertise that can step right in and just help carry the workload.

01:31:54.840 --> 01:31:58.600
 And the costs are all whatever the costs are, and they're passed on directly to the developer.

01:31:59.120 --> 01:32:00.260
 They pass through costs.

01:32:00.260 --> 01:32:10.000
 And that was the recommendation from finance was to try to lock up prices and things for the next five years.

01:32:11.140 --> 01:32:15.320
 And the idea was like, well, we keep coming back every one to two years with this.

01:32:16.540 --> 01:32:20.720
 So rather than have to do it frequently, let's lock it down for a little bit.

01:32:20.820 --> 01:32:29.800
 The other thing it does, too, is it gives the vendor an opportunity to recognize that they're going to have a certain level of workload that they're going to need to anticipate.

01:32:30.480 --> 01:32:35.960
 And I think that it locks them into helping us make sure that they're available to review those applications.

01:32:36.100 --> 01:32:36.260
 Right?

01:32:36.380 --> 01:32:36.500
 Yeah.

01:32:39.200 --> 01:32:41.520
 Okay, 13D is an agreement.

01:32:42.940 --> 01:32:43.500
 No questions?

